a =0.$ \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(5,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b04084c-8e69-5f53-a95d-664aba54aec9" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: Second or Third Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f76cb9a-40f1-5c3f-a9d4-4cf44f1d6914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f76cb9a-40f1-5c3f-a9d4-4cf44f1d6914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289722} :answer-field/id #uuid "429509a8-b66f-5a5e-ad6d-c517ad9ac724" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289721} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bedf116-1ecb-5f35-8da3-7700073366f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bedf116-1ecb-5f35-8da3-7700073366f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289724} :answer-field/id #uuid "898b994b-0c53-59f3-94e6-7fea00cc240e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ee566b5-e505-5e57-b184-f1dc97ae1c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-275806" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{3},1).$ Find the polar coordinates of $P.$ Give the polar angle in radians and do not round the answer.\n\nThe polar coordinates of $P$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{-\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi $ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(2,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "49bf1f94-d992-511d-9016-d471f2ad4358" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates for a Point on an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89a43567-80ae-55e1-9a80-0cab1f2218d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289727} {:answer/id #uuid "77ad02f0-2093-580b-997b-02f4b7542f56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12,\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289728} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c5c511-1cb3-5412-b140-03b591bc0629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289729} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c8d1f0-0f95-56dd-afaa-e576d639c8b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289730} {:answer/id #uuid "40a09d31-de61-57e6-914e-5150c1cd814b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89a43567-80ae-55e1-9a80-0cab1f2218d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(12,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289727} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa916477-017a-52ee-91fd-1472f6507a0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "501b4a98-609b-5b94-8408-270235db8b79" :question/math-academy-id "q-40756" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(0,12).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the positive half of $y$-axis, we have $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(12,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b04084c-8e69-5f53-a95d-664aba54aec9" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: Second or Third Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "315bd30f-100f-5493-9845-d4b7586d8c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\,k,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289734} {:answer/id #uuid "98c0aacd-a120-5a2c-8cab-44132e1a0161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(k,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289735} {:answer/id #uuid "15d30795-7a8b-53ee-aa2b-d3fc70fff641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\,k,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289736} {:answer/id #uuid "6bbf35e3-781a-5d5b-9ed7-714c40ca144d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{k},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289737} {:answer/id #uuid "a9be768a-d6e4-5ae0-9b7e-636ade1860ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{k},-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "315bd30f-100f-5493-9845-d4b7586d8c5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2}\\,k,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289734} :answer-field/id #uuid "105a8792-912e-5235-b08e-d4d278812fbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef027f0c-fc1b-5a90-a307-c36c568315d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-40761" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-k,k),$ where $k$ is a positive real number. What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-k)^{2}+{k}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{k}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-k}{k})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi $ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(\\sqrt{2}\\,k,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b04084c-8e69-5f53-a95d-664aba54aec9" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: Second or Third Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a29cd924-3229-5548-a6b6-5be340f39e0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(17,4.83)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289741} {:answer/id #uuid "05bc9bad-2150-594b-bfff-5020ba51fe43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(17,1.08)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289742} {:answer/id #uuid "379db828-6064-580a-9964-4902f87c7cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(17,4.22)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289743} {:answer/id #uuid "3369a72a-dc3d-59ab-a5a2-13f8340ae26b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(15,4.22)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289744} {:answer/id #uuid "62754e23-72c3-532d-b0e4-b6b888a94592" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(15,4.83)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "379db828-6064-580a-9964-4902f87c7cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(17,4.22)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289743} :answer-field/id #uuid "45e4850f-6935-5893-9683-eb7498e3a4e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11bcd9ef-1813-543c-b6f6-515ce882849c" :question/math-academy-id "q-8506" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-8,-15).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$ Round your answer to two decimal places where necessary." :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+(-15)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+225} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{289} \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-15}{-8})| \\\\  & ≈1.080\\,84.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the third quadrant, we add the reference angle to $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.080\\,84 \\\\  & ≈4.22,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. Therefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(17,4.22).$"}]}
